# Government Customer Requirements

When building features intended for government customers (federal, state, or local agencies),
the following requirements apply IN ADDITION to the normal project rules. Government work
carries legal and contractual obligations that exceed typical commercial standards.

## 1. Accessibility — Section 508 / WCAG 2.1 AA (Mandatory)

Section 508 of the Rehabilitation Act legally requires federal digital services to meet
WCAG 2.1 AA. This is non-negotiable for government work.

**Requirements:**
- Every interactive element must be fully operable by keyboard alone (no mouse-only flows).
- Visible focus indicators on all focusable elements. Never remove focus outlines without a
  compliant replacement.
- Proper semantic HTML (`<button>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, headings in order) and ARIA labels
  where semantics are insufficient.
- Color contrast must meet WCAG AA (4.5:1 normal text, 3:1 large text/UI). Prefer AAA where
  feasible. See `.builder/rules/theme-contrast-guidelines.mdc`.
- Never convey information by color alone — pair with text, icons, or patterns.
- All form fields must have associated labels and clear, programmatically-linked error
  messages. Use Carbon `FormField`/`TextInput` invalid props and `InlineNotification`.
- Test with a screen reader (VoiceOver/NVDA) and keyboard-only navigation before shipping.
- Carbon components are accessible by default — preserve that; do not strip ARIA attributes.

## 2. Security & Data Handling

- Never store PII or sensitive data in `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, cookies, or logs.
  The theming system uses `localStorage` for the `insureco-theme` key only — keep client
  storage limited to non-sensitive UI preferences.
- Reference all secrets via environment variables (e.g. `REPLACE_ENV.API_KEY`). Never hardcode
  keys, tokens, or credentials in source.
- Validate and sanitize all input at system boundaries (user input, external APIs) to prevent
  XSS, injection, and the OWASP Top 10.
- Do not log sensitive insurance data (SSNs, VINs, full addresses, DOB, policy/account numbers).
- Use HTTPS for all network requests; never downgrade to HTTP.

## 3. Privacy & Compliance

- Treat all insurance PII collected in flows (e.g. the sign-up flow: VINs, addresses, personal
  details) as sensitive. Collect only what is necessary.
- Be mindful of agency compliance posture (FISMA / FedRAMP for federal; state equivalents).
  When unsure whether a feature introduces a compliance concern, flag it rather than assume.
- Provide clear consent and disclosure points where personal data is collected.
- Avoid third-party scripts/trackers that exfiltrate user data without review.

## 4. Browser & Device Support

- Government environments are often locked down with older or restricted browsers.
- Define and respect a minimum support matrix; avoid bleeding-edge browser APIs without
  graceful fallbacks.
- Ensure the experience degrades gracefully and remains accessible on lower-spec devices.

## Checklist Before Shipping a Government-Facing Feature

- [ ] Full keyboard operability + visible focus on every interactive element
- [ ] Screen reader pass (labels, roles, reading order, error announcements)
- [ ] WCAG AA contrast verified in BOTH light and dark themes
- [ ] No information conveyed by color alone
- [ ] No PII/sensitive data in client storage or logs
- [ ] Secrets via env vars only; no hardcoded credentials
- [ ] Input validated/sanitized at boundaries
- [ ] Works on the supported (potentially older) browser matrix

